#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "textual>=8.2,<9",
# ]
#
# [tool.uv]
# exclude-newer = "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"
# ///

# Formatted with ruff's default settings: uvx ruff format <this file>

"""Pantheon: agent collaboration and convergence.

Each agent is a real CLI (claude, codex, or gemini; claude can also run on an ollama
model). A mode is a series of stages. A stage gives one role to one agent, or to every
agent at once, one per track. In implement, each track has its own architect, engineer,
and reviewer: the architect writes a design, the engineer builds it on a branch of its
own, and the reviewer reviews it, until the reviewer approves, the engineer refuses the
changes asked for, or the rounds run out. Then the project manager compares the builds,
picks one, and writes the report for you. Pantheon shows the team in a chat-room feed
(the room), gives each agent a tab with a live copy of its screen, and lets you
"transport" into an agent's own terminal when it needs you. Ctrl-] or the button on
tmux's status line brings you back. The top row has a tab for each session you have
open ({ and } switch them); the row below has its agents' tabs.

  pantheon                          open the session switcher
  pantheon start MODE [OPTIONS] [TASK]
                                    start a session, e.g. `pantheon start implement 123`
      -C, --dir DIR                 start in DIR (default: the current directory)
      -a, --agents N|SPEC,...       how many agents, or which ones in order
      --agent STAGE=SPEC            the agent for one single-agent stage
      --effort LEVEL                low, medium, high, max, or default (the tools' own)
      -y, --yolo, --no-yolo         start agents with permission checks off, or on;
                                    the yolo setting is the default
  pantheon start help [MODE]        list the modes, or show one mode's stages
  pantheon session SLUG             open a session (a unique part of the slug is enough)
  pantheon sessions [--prune DAYS]  list sessions; --prune deletes finished and old ones
  pantheon show SLUG [NAME]         print a session's room, or one of its results
  pantheon room SLUG [--last N]     print a session's room: its last 50 entries, or N
  pantheon say SLUG MESSAGE         post to a session's room; - reads it from stdin
  pantheon agents [MODE]            show the agents and models a mode would get here
  pantheon tools                    show the claude, codex, gemini, and tmux found now
  pantheon config show              list the settings, with yours marked
  pantheon config set KEY VALUE     change a setting (unset KEY...: back to default)
  pantheon update                   replace this program with the newest one on GitHub
  pantheon help [COMMAND]

SPEC is TOOL[:MODEL][@EFFORT], where TOOL is claude, codex, gemini, or ollama. -a N takes
the first N tools that can run here, in the order of the agents setting (claude, codex,
gemini, ollama); with no -a, a mode takes up to its own number (implement 3, hello 2).
The agents take the tracks in that order, and the first agent plays each single-agent
stage, unless the scramble setting gives each stage its own random order. Pantheon
passes no model or effort, so each tool's own config decides, unless a SPEC or an
effort level sets them. The levels are low (hello's), medium, high, and max; pantheon
agents shows what each one gives each tool, and the ollama_models setting names an
ollama model for each. Pantheon looks for the tools on PATH each time it starts;
disable_TOOL = true leaves one out. TASK is free text, an issue number (123, or #123 in
quotes for bash), or an issue URL.

Agents keep your own setup: agent files, skills, MCP servers, and permission rules.
Pantheon adds only rules that let them run the room's two commands without asking you.
An ollama agent is claude with the ollama server as its API; if claude keeps an API key
from a Console login, it sends that key to the server too.
Pantheon types each step's instructions straight into its agent, and the agent writes
its result to a file that ends with a "PANTHEON: <status>" line. The room is the team's
channel: each agent learns how to read it (pantheon room) and post to it (pantheon say),
and pantheon gives each post to every other agent, which decides whether it needs to
act. So an agent can ask the team a question, or wake another agent. You can post too
(ctrl+s): to answer, or to give a team that is done more work. Pantheon's own lines in
the room go to no agent. Each session has a private tmux server, so agents keep running
when pantheon exits. Claude and codex report through hooks; gemini reports through its
window title. The room names the model each agent really runs, and claude's effort,
where they differ from what pantheon asked for (gemini's model comes from its chat
file). Unless it runs from a clone of gregschmit/dotfiles (git updates that), pantheon
looks on GitHub for a newer version at most every 12 hours.

Files: ~/.local/state/pantheon (or $PANTHEON_HOME) holds config.toml and one sqlite file per
session, sessions/<slug>.sqlite3. That file is the whole session: its record, its lock,
the prompts, and the results. Agents write plain files, so while they run, pantheon
keeps their results in run/<slug>/, and copies each result into the session file.
pantheon say leaves each post in run/<slug>/posts/ for pantheon to give out.
"""

